#{[17592186532218 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532243 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532273 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532274 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 180\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532279 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532280 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532292 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532303 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532323 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532325 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532330 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532332 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-68 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532350 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532355 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532359 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 49\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532360 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532361 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532362 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}49 & -32\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532366 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532369 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532375 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532379 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532388 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532406 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532409 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 12 & 18\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532411 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532414 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 32 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532416 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532446 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532449 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 32 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532451 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532461 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532622 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{4}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532622 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={b}^{T}x.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of a function $f(x)={b}^{T}x$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532646 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186532646 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532648 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(2Ax+b)^{T}(2Ax+b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186532648 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=2Ax+b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2Ax+b)=2A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(2A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & =(2A)^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(2Ax+b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ -68\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532650 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532650 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532652 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186532652 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2+1+1 \\\\ 1+2-2 \\\\ 1-2+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532660 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186532660 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4-3 \\\\ 3+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532661 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532661 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532668 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{4}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532668 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={b}^{T}x.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of a function $f(x)={b}^{T}x$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532671 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532671 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532679 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>"] [17592186532679 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(2+3\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(2-3\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2+3\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2+3\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2+3\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ (2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+3{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t+{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532689 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186532689 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+0 \\\\ 0+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532692 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186532692 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6-8 \\\\ -4+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532704 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186532704 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=2{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 16 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-68 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532708 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(5Ax-b)^{T}(5Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186532708 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=5Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5Ax-b)=5A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =10\\cdot {A}^{T}(5Ax-b) \\\\  & =10\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 180\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532724 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532724 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186532929 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ k & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix})+((-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix})+((2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ k & 0\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (1)-(-1)\\cdot (0)))+((-2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (1)-(-1)\\cdot (k)))+((2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (3))+((-2)\\cdot (k-1))+((2)\\cdot (-3k))\\\\ &=(0)+(2-2k)+(-6k)\\\\ &=2-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-8k&=0\\\\ -8k&=-2\\\\ k&=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [17592186532943 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & k & 3 \\\\ -8 & -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix})+((4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -8 & -6\\end{vmatrix})+((-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & k \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-6)-(3)\\cdot (-4)))+((4)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (-6)-(3)\\cdot (-8)))+((-4)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (-4)-(k)\\cdot (-8)))\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot (12-6k))+((4)\\cdot (-6))+((-4)\\cdot (8k-20))\\\\ &=(12k-24)+(-24)+(80-32k)\\\\ &=32-20k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32-20k&=0\\\\ -20k&=-32\\\\ k&=\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{8}{5}.$"] [17592186532947 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 3 & -4 \\\\ k & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((-6)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix})+((-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & -3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{vmatrix})+((-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((-6)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (7)-(-3)\\cdot (8)))+((-3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (7)-(-3)\\cdot (-4)))+((-4)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (8)-(3)\\cdot (-4)))\\\\ &=((-6)\\cdot (45))+((-3)\\cdot (7k-12))+((-4)\\cdot (8k+12))\\\\ &=(-270)+(36-21k)+(-32k-48)\\\\ &=-53k-282\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-53k-282&=0\\\\ -53k&=282\\\\ k&=-\\frac{282}{53}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{282}{53}.$"] [17592186532948 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & k\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{vmatrix})+((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix})+((-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (k)-(0)\\cdot (-2)))+((0)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (k)-(0)\\cdot (1)))+((-1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (1)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (-2k))+((0)\\cdot (2k))+((-1)\\cdot (-2))\\\\ &=(-2k)+(0)+(2)\\\\ &=2-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2k&=0\\\\ -2k&=-2\\\\ k&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1.$"] [17592186532950 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -5 & 3 \\\\ k & -8 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -7 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((-5)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{vmatrix})+((5)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix})+((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & -8 \\\\ -3 & -7\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((-5)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (7)-(4)\\cdot (-7)))+((5)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (7)-(4)\\cdot (-3)))+((3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-7)-(-8)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((-5)\\cdot (-28))+((5)\\cdot (7k+12))+((3)\\cdot (-7k-24))\\\\ &=(140)+(35k+60)+(-21k-72)\\\\ &=14k+128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14k+128&=0\\\\ 14k&=-128\\\\ k&=-\\frac{64}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{64}{7}.$"] [17592186532952 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ k & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix})+((1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix})+((-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ k & 0\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-2)-(1)\\cdot (0)))+((1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(1)\\cdot (k)))+((-1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (0)-(0)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (0))+((1)\\cdot (-k-4))+((-1)\\cdot (0))\\\\ &=(0)+(-k-4)+(0)\\\\ &=-k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k-4&=0\\\\ -k&=4\\\\ k&=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-4.$"] [17592186532955 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & k \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix})+((-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & k \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix})+((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-2)-(k)\\cdot (-1)))+((-2)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-2)-(k)\\cdot (-3)))+((0)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-1)-(0)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot (k))+((-2)\\cdot (3k-6))+((0)\\cdot (-3))\\\\ &=(2k)+(12-6k)+(0)\\\\ &=12-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4k&=0\\\\ -4k&=-12\\\\ k&=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=3.$"] [17592186532959 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ k & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix})+((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix})+((-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))+((3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (0)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))+((-3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-2)-(0)\\cdot (-2)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (-4))+((3)\\cdot (-4))+((-3)\\cdot (-2k))\\\\ &=(-4)+(-12)+(6k)\\\\ &=6k-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k-16&=0\\\\ 6k&=16\\\\ k&=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{8}{3}.$"] [17592186532963 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -8 & 8 & k \\\\ -2 & 5 & -8\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & k \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{vmatrix})+((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-8 & k \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{vmatrix})+((5)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot ((8)\\cdot (-8)-(k)\\cdot (5)))+((3)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (-8)-(k)\\cdot (-2)))+((5)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (5)-(8)\\cdot (-2)))\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot (-5k-64))+((3)\\cdot (2k+64))+((5)\\cdot (-24))\\\\ &=(-15k-192)+(6k+192)+(-120)\\\\ &=-9k-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9k-120&=0\\\\ -9k&=120\\\\ k&=-\\frac{40}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{40}{3}.$"] [17592186532964 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 7 \\\\ k & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix})+((7)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ k & -3\\end{vmatrix})+((4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-3)-(7)\\cdot (-2)))+((7)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-3)-(7)\\cdot (k)))+((4)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot (20))+((7)\\cdot (-7k-6))+((4)\\cdot (2k-4))\\\\ &=(120)+(-49k-42)+(8k-16)\\\\ &=62-41k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62-41k&=0\\\\ -41k&=-62\\\\ k&=\\frac{62}{41}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{62}{41}.$"] [17592186533019 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2 uv + u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2 v + 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 v + 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 v + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 v + 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 2 v - 1,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2 uv + u - v} \\\\  & =⟨2 (2 uv + u - v),\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨4 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\cdot ⟨- 2 v - 1,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(4 uv + 2 u - 2 v)\\cdot (- 2 v - 1)+(2 uv)\\cdot (1 - 2 u)+(2 u + 2 v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 8 uv^{2} - 8 uv - 2 u + 4 v^{2} + 2 v- 4 u^{2} v + 2 uv+2 u + 2 v \\\\  & =- 4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv + 4 v^{2} + 4 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv + 4 v^{2} + 4 v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(- 4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv + 4 v^{2} + 4 v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533020 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- uv - u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 4,\\,-2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- v - 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & - u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & - v - 1 \\\\ 1 & - u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - v - 1 \\\\ 0 & - u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v + 1,\\,u + 1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- uv - u - v} \\\\  & =⟨(- uv - u - v),\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- uv - u - v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨- uv - u - v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\cdot ⟨v + 1,\\,u + 1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(- uv - u - v)\\cdot (v + 1)+(2 uv)\\cdot (u + 1)+(2 u + 2 v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- uv^{2} - 2 uv - u - v^{2} - v+2 u^{2} v + 2 uv+2 u + 2 v \\\\  & =2 u^{2} v - uv^{2} + u - v^{2} + v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2 u^{2} v - uv^{2} + u - v^{2} + v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(2 u^{2} v - uv^{2} + u - v^{2} + v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533024 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u^{2} + v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 u \\\\ 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 u \\\\ 0 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 2 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x + 2 y,\\,- x + y,\\,2 - z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u^{2} + v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,- u + v,\\,2 - (u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,- u + v,\\,- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,- u + v,\\,- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2⟩\\cdot ⟨- 2 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u + 2 v)\\cdot (- 2 u)+(- u + v)\\cdot (- 2 v)+(- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 2 u^{2} - 4 uv+2 uv - 2 v^{2}- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2 \\\\  & =- 3 u^{2} - 2 uv - 3 v^{2} + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x + 2 y,\\,- x + y,\\,2 - z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 3 u^{2} - 2 uv - 3 v^{2} + 2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}(- 3 u^{2} - 2 uv - 3 v^{2} + 2)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533026 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- 2 uv - u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- 2 v - 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- 2 u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & - 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & - 2 v - 1 \\\\ 1 & - 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & - 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v + 1,\\,2 u + 1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,x + y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- 2 uv - u - v} \\\\  & =⟨2 (- 2 uv - u - v),\\,2 uv,\\,u + v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 4 uv - 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,u + v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨- 4 uv - 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨2 v + 1,\\,2 u + 1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(- 4 uv - 2 u - 2 v)\\cdot (2 v + 1)+(2 uv)\\cdot (2 u + 1)+(u + v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 8 uv^{2} - 8 uv - 2 u - 4 v^{2} - 2 v+4 u^{2} v + 2 uv+u + v \\\\  & =4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv - u - 4 v^{2} - v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,x + y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv - u - 4 v^{2} - v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv - u - 4 v^{2} - v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533035 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2 uv - u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2 v - 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 v - 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1 - 2 v,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2 uv - u - v} \\\\  & =⟨(2 uv - u - v),\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 uv - u - v,\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨2 uv - u - v,\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨1 - 2 v,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(2 uv - u - v)\\cdot (1 - 2 v)+(uv)\\cdot (1 - 2 u)+(u + v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 4 uv^{2} + 4 uv - u + 2 v^{2} - v- 2 u^{2} v + uv+u + v \\\\  & =- 2 u^{2} v - 4 uv^{2} + 5 uv + 2 v^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 2 u^{2} v - 4 uv^{2} + 5 uv + 2 v^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(- 2 u^{2} v - 4 uv^{2} + 5 uv + 2 v^{2})\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533036 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- 2 u^{2} - v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 2 u^{2} - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- 4 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 2 u^{2} - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - 4 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & - 4 u \\\\ 1 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - 4 u \\\\ 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4 u,\\,2 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x + y,\\,2 x + y,\\,- z - 1⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- 2 u^{2} - v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨u + v,\\,2 u + v,\\,- (- 2 u^{2} - v^{2}) - 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u + v,\\,2 u + v,\\,2 u^{2} + v^{2} - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u + v,\\,2 u + v,\\,2 u^{2} + v^{2} - 1⟩\\cdot ⟨4 u,\\,2 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u + v)\\cdot (4 u)+(2 u + v)\\cdot (2 v)+(2 u^{2} + v^{2} - 1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =4 u^{2} + 4 uv+4 uv + 2 v^{2}+2 u^{2} + v^{2} - 1 \\\\  & =6 u^{2} + 8 uv + 3 v^{2} - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x + y,\\,2 x + y,\\,- z - 1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6 u^{2} + 8 uv + 3 v^{2} - 1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(6 u^{2} + 8 uv + 3 v^{2} - 1)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533048 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- u^{2} - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 4,\\,-2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- u^{2} - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- 2 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- u^{2} - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - 2 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & - 2 u \\\\ 1 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - 2 u \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 u,\\,4 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨3 x - y,\\,- x + 3 y,\\,z + 1⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- u^{2} - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- u + 3 v,\\,(- u^{2} - 2 v^{2}) + 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- u + 3 v,\\,- u^{2} - 2 v^{2} + 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- u + 3 v,\\,- u^{2} - 2 v^{2} + 1⟩\\cdot ⟨2 u,\\,4 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3 u - v)\\cdot (2 u)+(- u + 3 v)\\cdot (4 v)+(- u^{2} - 2 v^{2} + 1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =6 u^{2} - 2 uv- 4 uv + 12 v^{2}- u^{2} - 2 v^{2} + 1 \\\\  & =5 u^{2} - 6 uv + 10 v^{2} + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3 x - y,\\,- x + 3 y,\\,z + 1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5 u^{2} - 6 uv + 10 v^{2} + 1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(5 u^{2} - 6 uv + 10 v^{2} + 1)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533051 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2 u^{2} + v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 u \\\\ 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 u \\\\ 0 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 4 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x + 2 y,\\,2 x + 3 y,\\,2 z - 2⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2 u^{2} + v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,2 u + 3 v,\\,2 (2 u^{2} + v^{2}) - 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,2 u + 3 v,\\,4 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,2 u + 3 v,\\,4 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2⟩\\cdot ⟨- 4 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u + 2 v)\\cdot (- 4 u)+(2 u + 3 v)\\cdot (- 2 v)+(4 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 4 u^{2} - 8 uv- 4 uv - 6 v^{2}+4 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2 \\\\  & =- 12 uv - 4 v^{2} - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x + 2 y,\\,2 x + 3 y,\\,2 z - 2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 12 uv - 4 v^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(- 12 uv - 4 v^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533059 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 u \\\\ 1 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 u \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 4 u,\\,4 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨3 x - y,\\,- 2 x + y,\\,- z - 2⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- 2 u + v,\\,- (2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}) - 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- 2 u + v,\\,- 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- 2 u + v,\\,- 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2⟩\\cdot ⟨- 4 u,\\,4 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3 u - v)\\cdot (- 4 u)+(- 2 u + v)\\cdot (4 v)+(- 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 12 u^{2} + 4 uv- 8 uv + 4 v^{2}- 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2 \\\\  & =- 14 u^{2} - 4 uv + 6 v^{2} - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3 x - y,\\,- 2 x + y,\\,- z - 2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 14 u^{2} - 4 uv + 6 v^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(- 14 u^{2} - 4 uv + 6 v^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533067 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,uv + u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v + 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v + 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v + 1 \\\\ 1 & u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & v + 1 \\\\ 0 & u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- v - 1,\\,1 - u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2 z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=uv + u - v} \\\\  & =⟨2 (uv + u - v),\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨2 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨- v - 1,\\,1 - u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(2 uv + 2 u - 2 v)\\cdot (- v - 1)+(uv)\\cdot (1 - u)+(u + v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 2 uv^{2} - 4 uv - 2 u + 2 v^{2} + 2 v- u^{2} v + uv+u + v \\\\  & =- u^{2} v - 2 uv^{2} - 3 uv - u + 2 v^{2} + 3 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2 z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- u^{2} v - 2 uv^{2} - 3 uv - u + 2 v^{2} + 3 v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}(- u^{2} v - 2 uv^{2} - 3 uv - u + 2 v^{2} + 3 v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533089 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot (-5)-(-3)\\cdot 1)i-(4\\cdot (-5)-(-3)\\cdot (-1))j+(4\\cdot 1-(-5)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =28i+23j-k \\\\  & =⟨28,23,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{28}^{2}+{23}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{784+529+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1314} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{146}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186533101 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\times q & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(6\\cdot (-6)-0\\cdot 3)i-((-3)\\cdot (-6)-0\\cdot 4)j+((-3)\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =-36i-18j-33k \\\\  & =⟨-36,-18,-33⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,p\\times q\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-36)^{2}+(-18)^{2}+(-33)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1296+324+1089} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2709} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{301}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{301}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186533107 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\times t & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 3)i-((-5)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 6)j+((-5)\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 6)k \\\\  & =-23i+20j-39k \\\\  & =⟨-23,20,-39⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,r\\times t\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-23)^{2}+{20}^{2}+(-39)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{529+400+1521} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2450} \\\\  & =35\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186533110 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot (-1))i-((-5)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 1)j+((-5)\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-13i-17j+k \\\\  & =⟨-13,-17,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-13)^{2}+(-17)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169+289+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{459} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{51}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186533112 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\times t & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(6\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 6)i-(4\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 2)j+(4\\cdot 6-6\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-36i+22j+12k \\\\  & =⟨-36,22,12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,r\\times t\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-36)^{2}+{22}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1296+484+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1924} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{481}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{481}}{2}=\\sqrt{481}.\n$$"] [17592186533116 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\times t & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 1)i-(5\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 2)j+(5\\cdot 1-(-5)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =9i-3j+15k \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,15⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,r\\times t\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{15}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81+9+225} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{315} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{35}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186533147 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -84 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -14\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533155 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533162 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533174 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 14 & 10 \\\\ -15 & -35\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533176 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533178 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 10 & -15 & -35\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533181 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533195 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533211 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533215 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533222 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533229 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"]}
